# .dockerignore — what NOT to send to the Docker daemon.
#
# When you run `docker build .`, the CLI packages up the "build context" (that
# `.` — this whole directory) and ships it to the daemon BEFORE any instruction
# runs. Everything listed here is excluded from that package.
#
# Three reasons it matters:
#
#  1. SPEED. A context with .git and stray binaries can be hundreds of MB, and
#     it's transferred on every single build.
#  2. CACHE CORRECTNESS. `COPY . .` invalidates its layer whenever ANY file in
#     the context changes. Without this file, editing a README would bust the
#     cache and trigger a full recompile.
#  3. SECRETS. Anything copied into an image is IN the image — recoverable by
#     anyone who pulls it, even if a later layer deletes it. A .env that sneaks
#     in is a leaked credential.

# Version control — large, and never needed to compile
.git
.gitignore

# Local secrets — must never enter an image
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# Build artifacts (we compile INSIDE the image; host binaries are the wrong
# OS/arch anyway and would just bloat the context)
*.exe
*.out
api
loadtest

# Docs and editor noise
*.md
.vscode
.idea

# Docker's own files — not needed inside the build
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml

# NOTE what is deliberately NOT ignored:
#   docs/     — the generated Swagger package is imported by cmd/api, so the
#               build FAILS without it. Easy mistake to make.
#   go.sum    — required for reproducible, verified dependency downloads.
